366	// An ABSORBED pack's entry, reshaped for the pack that is about to swallow it.
367	// Two things a plain id rename cannot get right on its own:
368	//
369	//   CONFIG COLLIDES. Both entries carry a flat `config`, and the merge below
370	//   spreads one over the other — so an absorbed `{ rules: [...] }` would land as
371	//   the survivor's own `config.rules`, a key the survivor may already mean
372	//   something else by. Nesting it under the absorbed pack's own id keeps the
373	//   parameters saying whose they are, which is also what the survivor's code then
374	//   reads.
375	//   ANSWERS OUTLIVE THEIR QUESTION. An answer is recorded against a question the
376	//   absorbed pack declared; the survivor does not declare it, so the answer
377	//   becomes an interview-hygiene finding the owner cannot act on. The record names
378	//   the ids it drops rather than dropping every answer, because a RENAME (as
379	//   opposed to an absorption) keeps asking its questions.
